<Base title="Under Whose Rule"
      eyebrow="Three hundred years, six regimes, one parish"
      dek="Arienzo did not sit still under one flag while this family was in it. Spanish viceroys, Austrians, Bourbons, the French, the Bourbons again, then Italy — and four kilometres away, across a border, a papal enclave. The bands are who governed the place; the red is this family's own records; the ochre is what the town was living through.">

  <UnderWhoseRule />

  <h2>Why this is on an archive of one family</h2>
  <p>Because the regimes made the records. Everything this archive can read about the Falco before
  1809 comes from a parish priest, because the parish was the only institution writing anything down.
  Then the French arrived, abolished feudalism, and in <strong>1809</strong> ordered civil registration
  — births, marriages and deaths recorded by the mayor, in duplicate, on printed forms, with the
  parents of the deceased named in every death act.</p>
  <p>That single administrative decision is the reason <a href={u("/antenati")}>Pasquale Falco's death
  act</a> exists and can be read two hundred years later. The parish books say a man died; the civil
  act says whose son he was. The whole spine of this archive above generation three rests on a
  Napoleonic filing requirement.</p>
  <p>The Bourbons came back in 1815 and kept the system, which is why the volumes are catalogued as
  <em>Stato civile della restaurazione</em> — the restoration's civil registers. The name on the
  spine of the book is a regime change.</p>

  <h2>What this page is, and is not</h2>
  <p class="small"><strong>The red markers are evidence</strong> — every one is a document reproduced
  or cited elsewhere on this site. <strong>The bands and the ochre markers are not.</strong> They are
  ordinary published history, put here as context so the family's dates can be read against the world
  that produced them. Where the two touch — 1809, 1935, 1955 — that is said explicitly.</p>
  <p class="small">One local detail worth its own line: the 1831 death acts give Arienzo's district as
  <strong>Nola</strong>, and the 1832 acts onward give <strong>Caserta</strong>. The district appears
  to have been redrawn between them. Observed in the registers; not yet verified against an
  administrative source.</p>
</Base>
